  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Consider a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> uniform circular disk of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>. It is pin supported at its center and is at rest initially. The disk is acted upon by a constant force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> through a massless string wrapped around its periphery as shown in the figure.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0122_ae53c2ac6ebb.png\" style=\"width: 178px; height: 218px;\" /></p><p>Suppose the disk makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math> number of revolutions to attain an angular speed of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>rad</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math>, to the nearest integer, is _______ .</p><p>[Given : In one complete revolution, the disk rotates by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>28</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>rad</mi></math>]</p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "20",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>&#964;</mi><mi>I</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>.</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>rad</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>&#969;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>&#969;</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#945;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><mi>&#952;</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced><mn>50</mn></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mn>10</mn></mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2500</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></math><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>125</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>rad</mi></math></p><p>No. of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>125</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#8776;</mo><mn>20</mn></math> revolution</p>"
  }
}